{"action":"revised","summary":"Wrote the complete first draft of the application for the self-flushing inline liquid metering valve: all ten draft/ body files, a 29-entry numeral table, and four figure specifications (whole-valve section, metering-detail section in the fill position, rinse-detail section on the return stroke, and an index-collar detail). Claims are 20 in two statutory classes: apparatus claim 1 with dependents 2 to 12, and method claim 13 with dependents 14 to 20. Note on the art: prior_art/INDEX.md lists no references, so the Background carries no citations and the art you gave me may be incomplete; the claims were drafted against the disclosure and the general problem statement rather than against searched references, and a real search could change what claim 1 should recite.","reasoning":["Claim 1 recites three ordered relationships rather than one, because the ordering is what the invention actually is: the concentrate port closes before the downstream land uncovers the admission port, and the follower pin on the piston rod opens the flush poppet on the return stroke with the poppet closing before the waist reconnects the concentrate port. A spool with two lands and a waist, a check valve on the outlet, and a rinse line are each ordinary in isolation; what is not ordinary is that the rinse is gated by the element that carries the metering piston, so the rinse cannot be commanded late, skipped, or performed while the concentrate port is open. That coupling is the feature I would argue from, and it is stated in the Summary and the Detailed Description before it is needed.","I deliberately kept the whole indexing mechanism out of claim 1. The index collar, helical cam groove, three stop faces, detent, window and indicia are the most visually distinctive part of the disclosure, but none of them is needed to make the metering and self-rinse sequence work, so putting them in claim 1 would have given away scope for nothing. They form the second rung of the ladder at claims 6 to 9 and 17 to 19, where each adds a feature that can be argued on its own: self-indexing from the stroke, travel-limited dose volumes, mechanical retention, and mechanical display.","Claim 1 says 'at least one rinse opening' although the disclosure describes two. The two-opening arrangement, with one aimed across the concentrate port and one across the one-way outlet valve, is the version that solves the residue problem the Background sets up, so it sits in claims 2 and 15 as the next-narrowest fallback rather than as a limitation on the broadest claim.","Claims 4, 5 and 16 exist as separate rungs because they capture the two consequences of the geometry that are easiest to defend and hardest to design around: no piston position places the concentrate source in communication with the carrier path, and the concentrate port stays shut throughout the rinse interval. Both are supported in the same words in the Detailed Description.","The cartridge and threaded end cap are claimed at 10, 11 and 20 rather than in claim 1. They are a serviceability embodiment and a different infringement story (the replacement cartridge itself), so they belong in their own rung, and the method claim ends with the cartridge exchange step for that reason.","The disclosure fixes the ordering of the ports and lands but not the axial location of the flush poppet, the direction the piston travels to discharge, or how the rod is driven. I resolved only what the drawings could not avoid resolving, and I resolved it in the figure briefs rather than in the specification: the spec says the poppet is normally closed and connected to the flush bypass and that the pin opens it near the end of the return, while the drawing places the poppet where the pin can physically reach it. No invented location, dimension, material value, or performance figure entered the patent text or the claims.","Numerals were allocated to keep every sheet at or under eight. FIG. 1 carries the assembly, housing, ports, sleeve, window, cartridge and end cap; FIG. 2 carries the metering geometry; FIG. 3 carries the rinse circuit; FIG. 4 carries the indexing parts. Where a part had to appear on a sheet that does not number it, the brief describes it generically as a hatched band, a plain shaft, an unnumbered ring or a ball on a seat, so no figure shows a part it does not list. Figure references in the Detailed Description were rewritten so that each 'FIG. n shows' sentence names only numerals that appear on that sheet.","The external concentrate source was left unnumbered. It is not part of the valve, it is drawn only as a short schematic stub that ends inside the drawing area, and numbering it would have cost a slot on a sheet that needed it for claimed structure.","The Background carries no citations because prior_art/INDEX.md lists no references. It states the technical problems in neutral terms, carryover residue between doses, dependence on command ordering for liquid separation, and the fragility of screw or electronic dose settings, so that the later arguments about the ordered port geometry and the stroke-driven rinse have somewhere in the specification to attach."],"changes":["draft/01-title.md: title set from the drafting brief.","draft/02-cross-reference.md and draft/03-government-support.md: 'Not applicable.' as instructed, sections retained.","draft/04-field.md: field statement covering inline metering with same-cycle rinse.","draft/05-background.md: problem statement on liquid separation, residue and carryover, manual cleaning, and screw or electronic dose settings; no citations, as no references were supplied.","draft/06-summary.md: summary of the housing, sleeve, ports, spool piston, ordered isolation, the stroke-driven rinse circuit, the indexing dose selection and the cartridge.","draft/07-drawings.md: brief description of four figures.","draft/08-detailed-description.md: full description keyed to the numerals, including the ordered port and land sequence, the rinse path and its timing, the index collar and mechanical display, the cartridge, materials and alternatives, and a full operation walkthrough matching the method claim.","draft/09-claims.md: 20 claims, apparatus 1 to 12 and method 13 to 20, two independent claims.","draft/10-abstract.md: single-paragraph abstract within 150 words, no citations.","draft/numerals.md: 29 numerals, each once against one part name.","figures/fig-01.md: longitudinal section of the whole valve, numerals 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 52, 64, 66.","figures/fig-02.md: enlarged metering section in the fill position, numerals 18, 20, 22, 24, 30, 32, 34, 38.","figures/fig-03.md: enlarged rinse detail on the return stroke with the poppet held open, numerals 22, 26, 28, 44, 56, 58, 60, 62.","figures/fig-04.md: fragmentary side view of the index collar and dose-setting parts, numerals 36, 40, 42, 44, 46, 48, 50, 54."],"prior_art_strategy":"prior_art/INDEX.md lists no references, so there was nothing to read around and nothing to cite; the Background therefore contains no [REF:] citations, and I made no statement about any specific document. In place of reference-by-reference distinguishing, I built claim 1 on the combination that the disclosure itself treats as the point of the invention and that is least likely to be met by a single conventional doser: the axial ordering of land faces and port edges that makes concentrate-to-carrier isolation a geometric property rather than a control-sequence property, together with a rinse circuit whose gate is opened by the follower pin on the piston rod during the return stroke and closed again before the waist reconnects the concentrate port. A metering spool, a check valve on a dose outlet, and a flushing line are individually familiar, so the claim earns its scope from the timing relationships among them, and the Detailed Description explains the technical problem each relationship solves (back-drive toward the concentrate source, drift from retained residue, and a rinse that can be deferred or skipped) so that the argument exists in the specification before it is needed. The dependent ladder retreats first to the two aimed rinse openings and the wiping lip, then to the self-indexing collar and its three mechanically retained and displayed settings, then to the cartridge and end cap. Because no search was run, treat this as a provisional shape for the claims: a real search may show that claim 1 must take on the wiping lip, the aimed rinse openings, or the indexing collar to stay clear of the art.","questions":[],"answer":"The draft is written and internally consistent. draft/ holds the complete application: title, cross-reference and government-support sections marked "Not applicable.", field, background, summary, brief description of the drawings, detailed description, 20 claims, and abstract. draft/numerals.md holds 29 numerals, each appearing once against one part name and each appearing on at least one sheet. figures/ holds four self-contained briefs: FIG. 1 a longitudinal section of the whole valve, FIG. 2 an enlarged metering section in the fill position, FIG. 3 an enlarged rinse detail during the return stroke with the flush poppet held open, and FIG. 4 a fragmentary side view of the index collar and the parts that set and retain the dose. Each brief lists exactly the numerals on its sheet, keeps every sheet to eight numerals or fewer, and stays at or below 2800 characters.

Claim 1 is an apparatus claim built on the ordered port and land geometry plus the stroke-driven rinse: the concentrate port closes before the downstream sealing land uncovers the carrier-liquid admission port, and the follower pin on the piston rod opens the normally closed flush poppet during the return stroke, the poppet closing before the waist reconnects the concentrate port. Claim 13 is the corresponding method. The indexing collar, the three stop faces, the detent, the window, the wiping lip, the two aimed rinse openings and the cartridge are held back as dependent rungs so that claim 1 carries no limitation it does not need.

One thing to weigh before filing: prior_art/INDEX.md is empty, so no search results informed this draft and no reference could be cited in the Background. The claims were shaped against the disclosure and the general problem, not against known art, so the scope of claim 1 should be revisited once a search has been run."}

